1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,086
Putin felt
that Russia faced an existential threat.

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:04,921
He had to deal with that threat.

3
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:07,882
And the United States
and the Europeans were doing

4
00:00:07,882 --> 00:00:12,012
nothing to negotiate a way to avoid war.

5
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:16,683
The West is leading
Ukraine down the primrose

6
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,854
path, and the Israelis
are executing a genocide in Gaza.

7
00:00:20,895 --> 00:00:24,566
It's not China's fault
that it wants to be powerful.

8
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:28,903
So if you get into trouble
and you dial 911,

9
00:00:28,903 --> 00:00:31,906
there's nobody at the other end.

10
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:35,994
Hi, this is John Mearsheimer,
and I'm here today

11
00:00:35,994 --> 00:00:40,999
to make my first video
on my new YouTube channel.

12
00:00:41,332 --> 00:00:47,464
And the subject I'd like to talk about
is the origins of the war in Ukraine.

13
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:52,343
That war began on February 24th of 2022.

14
00:00:52,385 --> 00:00:54,971
And of course, the war continues on.

15
00:00:54,971 --> 00:00:58,141
Indeed, it's been longer than World
War One.

16
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:01,144
And I want to talk about its origins.

17
00:01:01,186 --> 00:01:05,732
And in particular,
I'm interested in the question of why

18
00:01:06,066 --> 00:01:09,402
the Russians were not deterred

19
00:01:09,402 --> 00:01:12,906
and why they actually attacked on

20
00:01:13,239 --> 00:01:16,868
February 24th, 2022.

21
00:01:16,868 --> 00:01:21,289
And this is essentially a question
of conventional deterrence, because

22
00:01:21,289 --> 00:01:26,002
the attack was a conventional attack,
nuclear weapons were not involved here.

23
00:01:26,044 --> 00:01:28,296
Nuclear deterrence was not at play.

24
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:30,423
It's a question
of conventional deterrence.

25
00:01:30,423 --> 00:01:35,428
And again,
why were the Russians not deterred?

26
00:01:35,470 --> 00:01:38,473
Now, this is a very interesting
subject to me

27
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:43,311
because the war is important,
but also because I actually wrote a book

28
00:01:43,353 --> 00:01:47,732
on conventional deterrence
during the Cold War.

29
00:01:48,149 --> 00:01:52,695
The whole question of whether or not
the Soviet Union

30
00:01:52,737 --> 00:01:55,907
or more generally,
the Warsaw Pact would attack

31
00:01:55,907 --> 00:02:02,288
NATO was an issue of great importance,
and people paid attention to the question

32
00:02:02,288 --> 00:02:06,251
of when or under what circumstances

33
00:02:06,251 --> 00:02:10,338
the Russians might attack against NATO.

34
00:02:10,338 --> 00:02:16,427
And I actually wrote my PhD dissertation
on the subject of conventional deterrence.

35
00:02:16,427 --> 00:02:20,515
And my first book,
which grew out of my dissertation,

36
00:02:20,515 --> 00:02:23,184
is entitled Conventional Deterrence.

37
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:25,979
So I've thought a lot about this subject.

38
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:29,274
And because the NATO Warsaw Pact

39
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:32,986
situation from the Cold War,
Cold War bears

40
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:38,575
a lot of resemblance 
to the Russian decision to invade Ukraine.

41
00:02:38,575 --> 00:02:42,871
I think I have a lot to say, or
I should say, I think my theory has a lot

42
00:02:42,912 --> 00:02:46,875
to say
about the origins of the Ukraine war.

43
00:02:46,875 --> 00:02:51,004
So what I'd like to do today is I'd first

44
00:02:51,004 --> 00:02:54,883
like to lay out my theory
of conventional deterrence.

45
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:58,928
Leaving aside the whole question
of the Ukraine war, just explain to you

46
00:02:58,928 --> 00:03:02,640
how I think about conventional deterrence.

47
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:06,603
And then once I finish that task,
switch gears

48
00:03:06,603 --> 00:03:09,689
and talk about the Ukraine war, talk

49
00:03:09,731 --> 00:03:16,571
about what happened on February 24th, 2022
and what has happened

50
00:03:16,613 --> 00:03:20,825
subsequently and in essence, marry

51
00:03:20,825 --> 00:03:25,246
my theory of conventional deterrence
to the Ukraine war.

52
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:30,752
Now, let me start by talking
about conventional deterrence.

53
00:03:30,752 --> 00:03:35,256
And the first point I want to make,
and this is a terribly important point,

54
00:03:35,256 --> 00:03:39,802
is that when you think about
conventional deterrence, you have to focus

55
00:03:39,802 --> 00:03:44,307
on the political variable
and the military variable.

56
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:47,810
And you're probably saying to yourself,
what exactly does he mean?

57
00:03:47,852 --> 00:03:50,855
When I talk about the political variable,
it's very,

58
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:55,235
very important to understand
that there are political forces

59
00:03:55,235 --> 00:03:59,656
that push a state to go to war,
and the question of how powerful

60
00:03:59,656 --> 00:04:05,161
those political forces are really matters
for purposes of understanding deterrence.

61
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:08,373
So you have this political variable,

62
00:04:08,414 --> 00:04:11,793
and then you have a military variable.

63
00:04:11,793 --> 00:04:15,713
And the military variable is all about how

64
00:04:15,713 --> 00:04:18,800
well you will do

65
00:04:18,841 --> 00:04:22,804
on the battlefield
once you're engaged in the fight

66
00:04:22,804 --> 00:04:27,517
militarily, how likely are you to succeed?

67
00:04:27,558 --> 00:04:30,603
What sorts of costs will you have to pay?

68
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:33,564
That's
what the military variable is all about.

69
00:04:33,564 --> 00:04:37,443
The political variable deals
with those broader political forces

70
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:41,322
that are pushing you to contemplate
going to war,

71
00:04:41,322 --> 00:04:45,118
and it's very important to understand
that you have to take into account

72
00:04:45,159 --> 00:04:47,829
both the military
and the political variable.

73
00:04:47,829 --> 00:04:51,833
And this, by the way,
this relationship between politics

74
00:04:51,833 --> 00:04:55,461
and military considerations is reflected

75
00:04:55,461 --> 00:04:58,589
in Clausewitz's very famous dictum

76
00:04:58,756 --> 00:05:03,886
that war is an extension of politics
by other means.

77
00:05:03,886 --> 00:05:09,350
War, meaning the military variable, is
an extension of politics by other means.

78
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:12,562
So we have to always keep in mind
that there are

79
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:16,316
these two variables at play,
and we have to think not only about

80
00:05:16,316 --> 00:05:20,278
each variable by itself,
but how they relate to each other.

81
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:24,449
Now, let me start by talking
about the military variable.

82
00:05:25,074 --> 00:05:31,414
My argument is that when states think
about going to war, and here we're talking

83
00:05:31,414 --> 00:05:34,959
about a large scale conventional war,
you want to keep that in mind.

84
00:05:35,001 --> 00:05:38,129
Remember
I said before, what motivated me to study

85
00:05:38,129 --> 00:05:41,299
this issue was the NATO Warsaw Pact

86
00:05:41,341 --> 00:05:45,303
conventional balance during the Cold War.

87
00:05:45,303 --> 00:05:49,724
And here we're talking
about the balance of power between

88
00:05:49,724 --> 00:05:53,603
Russia's conventional forces
and Ukraine's conventional forces.

89
00:05:53,603 --> 00:05:57,231
Back in February of 2022.

90
00:05:57,523 --> 00:06:01,402
So when a country,
whether it's the Warsaw Pact,

91
00:06:01,444 --> 00:06:04,364
thinking about attacking NATO or Russia,
thinking

92
00:06:04,364 --> 00:06:07,575
about attacking Ukraine

93
00:06:08,534 --> 00:06:10,536
when it thinks about the

94
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:15,958
military considerations at play,
my argument

95
00:06:15,958 --> 00:06:19,837
is it focuses on the particular strategy

96
00:06:19,837 --> 00:06:23,841
that it might employ to win the war.

97
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:29,305
And my argument is that a state that's
thinking about attacking another state

98
00:06:29,305 --> 00:06:35,144
in a major conventional war has three
strategies that it can choose among.

99
00:06:35,186 --> 00:06:39,315
What is what I call
blitzkrieg two is what I call attrition

100
00:06:39,315 --> 00:06:44,153
strategy, and three is what I call
a limited Aim strategy.

101
00:06:44,195 --> 00:06:49,200
Now, let me unpack what I mean
when I talk about each one of those

102
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:54,914
strategies and attrition strategy
and the blitzkrieg strategy

103
00:06:55,123 --> 00:07:00,670
of both aimed
at producing a decisive victory,

104
00:07:01,295 --> 00:07:05,091
a blitzkrieg is designed to produce

105
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:08,678
a quick and decisive victory.

106
00:07:08,886 --> 00:07:14,225
Think about the Germans
invading France in 1940.

107
00:07:14,267 --> 00:07:18,771
On May 10th, 1940, the Wehrmacht invades
France.

108
00:07:18,771 --> 00:07:24,152
It quickly overruns France
and then pushes the British Army off the

109
00:07:24,193 --> 00:07:25,695
beaches at Dunkirk.

110
00:07:25,695 --> 00:07:28,781
This is a classic blitzkrieg.

111
00:07:28,948 --> 00:07:35,913
Blitzkrieg produce quick and decisive
victories and attrition.

112
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:39,667
Strategy, in contrast, produces

113
00:07:39,709 --> 00:07:44,547
a slow, a protracted but decisive victory.

114
00:07:44,547 --> 00:07:48,426
So it's important to understand that
with both a blitzkrieg

115
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:54,015
and an attrition strategy,
you are pursuing decisive victory,

116
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:59,479
defeating the other side almost,
if not completely.

117
00:08:00,313 --> 00:08:03,900
The third strategy is a limited
aim strategy,

118
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:08,738
and there a state
does not pursue a decisive victory.

119
00:08:08,779 --> 00:08:13,326
What it does
is it simply conquers territory

120
00:08:13,326 --> 00:08:16,829
in that rival state.

121
00:08:16,871 --> 00:08:21,709
It goes to war and lops off

122
00:08:21,709 --> 00:08:26,506
or conquers
a small piece of the other side's

123
00:08:26,506 --> 00:08:30,843
territory,
and then it moves to a defensive strategy.

124
00:08:30,885 --> 00:08:34,388
This is why it's called a limited
aim strategy,

125
00:08:34,388 --> 00:08:39,727
and not a strategy
that pursues decisive ends,

126
00:08:39,727 --> 00:08:45,483
which is what blitzkrieg
and an attrition strategy do.

127
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:51,364
So these are the three strategies
that a state can choose among.

128
00:08:51,447 --> 00:08:54,909
Now how do we rank order these strategies?

129
00:08:54,909 --> 00:08:58,454
What are these
three strategies say about the likelihood

130
00:08:58,454 --> 00:09:02,124
of deterrence succeeding or failing?

131
00:09:02,458 --> 00:09:06,003
My argument is that if a state can

132
00:09:06,003 --> 00:09:08,965
launch a blitzkrieg and win.

133
00:09:08,965 --> 00:09:10,758
Let me put it slightly differently.

134
00:09:10,758 --> 00:09:14,220
If a state thinks it can
launch a blitzkrieg,

135
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:19,559
that will produce a quick and decisive
victory, deterrence will fail.

136
00:09:19,934 --> 00:09:24,438
If it thinks that it has to launch
an attrition strategy.

137
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:26,482
It can't win quickly.

138
00:09:26,482 --> 00:09:30,319
It's aiming to win decisively,
but it can't win quickly.

139
00:09:30,361 --> 00:09:33,030
Then it will not attack.

140
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:35,533
So an attrition strategy

141
00:09:36,659 --> 00:09:38,619
promotes deterrence,

142
00:09:38,619 --> 00:09:43,874
and a blitzkrieg strategy promotes
the failure of deterrence.

143
00:09:44,041 --> 00:09:45,751
Just to go to World War Two.

144
00:09:45,751 --> 00:09:50,298
One of the principal reasons
that the Germans invaded

145
00:09:50,298 --> 00:09:54,552
France in May of 1940
was that they had a blitzkrieg strategy,

146
00:09:54,552 --> 00:09:58,598
which they thought would produce a quick
and decisive victory.

147
00:09:58,598 --> 00:10:00,266
And that's why they went to war.

148
00:10:00,266 --> 00:10:05,021
And they were successful
when the Germans invaded the Soviet Union.

149
00:10:05,062 --> 00:10:08,858
A year later, this is on June 22nd, 1941.

150
00:10:08,941 --> 00:10:14,864
They thought that the blitzkrieg strategy
that they had ready to go

151
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:19,702
would produce a quick and decisive victory
inside the Soviet Union.

152
00:10:19,744 --> 00:10:24,457
Of course, it failed in the Soviet case,
and the war

153
00:10:24,707 --> 00:10:28,628
evolved into a war of attrition.

154
00:10:29,086 --> 00:10:33,424
Very important to understand that
the Germans did not go into the Soviet

155
00:10:33,424 --> 00:10:37,553
Union in 1941, thinking
they would end up in a war of attrition.

156
00:10:37,595 --> 00:10:41,265
If that were the case, I would argue
they would not have attacked.

157
00:10:41,307 --> 00:10:45,102
They attacked because they thought
they had a blitzkrieg strategy

158
00:10:45,102 --> 00:10:49,649
that would provide a war winning strategy.

159
00:10:49,732 --> 00:10:52,109
So you see that blitzkrieg.

160
00:10:52,109 --> 00:10:55,321
Let's create because it

161
00:10:55,321 --> 00:11:00,493
produces or promises
to produce a quick and decisive victory

162
00:11:00,534 --> 00:11:05,748
is a formula for deterrence failure.

163
00:11:05,748 --> 00:11:09,627
And I would argue, by the way,
that during the Cold War, the Soviets

164
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:12,963
and their Warsaw Pact allies
never gave any thought

165
00:11:12,963 --> 00:11:16,050
of attacking into Western Europe
because they understood

166
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:20,638
that they could not execute a blitzkrieg
against the NATO forces

167
00:11:20,638 --> 00:11:23,641
that would be arrayed
against them in the fight.

168
00:11:23,849 --> 00:11:26,644
And that's why deterrence was so robust.

169
00:11:26,644 --> 00:11:31,190
This is not to deny, of course,
that nuclear deterrence mattered as well.

170
00:11:31,524 --> 00:11:34,819
Now let's go to the limited Aim strategy.

171
00:11:35,444 --> 00:11:40,991
The limited Aim strategy
is the easiest strategy to execute

172
00:11:41,033 --> 00:11:45,371
because it is limited,
which means that all you're doing

173
00:11:45,371 --> 00:11:50,334
is grabbing some territory
from your adversary.

174
00:11:50,376 --> 00:11:54,422
You're not attempting
to defeat your adversary decisively.

175
00:11:54,422 --> 00:11:56,632
It is a limited strategy.

176
00:11:56,632 --> 00:12:01,053
There are limited aims at play,
so it is the easiest

177
00:12:01,053 --> 00:12:04,056
of the three strategies
to execute for sure.

178
00:12:04,473 --> 00:12:09,478
But actually it is not ultimately
an attractive strategy.

179
00:12:09,478 --> 00:12:14,984
And the reason that it is ultimately not
an attractive strategy is

180
00:12:14,984 --> 00:12:18,362
because you don't defeat the other side
decisively,

181
00:12:18,362 --> 00:12:22,324
and invariably the other side

182
00:12:22,533 --> 00:12:29,081
will continue the fight
and you will end up in a war of attrition.

183
00:12:29,498 --> 00:12:31,625
So it's very important to understand that.

184
00:12:31,625 --> 00:12:36,130
What I'm saying here is that
if you launch a blitzkrieg and it fails,

185
00:12:36,172 --> 00:12:38,466
you'll end up in a war of attrition.

186
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:43,345
And if you launch a limited Aim strategy,
you'll be successful at first,

187
00:12:43,429 --> 00:12:46,557
but it will probably end up

188
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:49,643
turning in to a war of attrition.

189
00:12:49,643 --> 00:12:52,062
And nobody wants a war of attrition.

190
00:12:52,062 --> 00:12:57,943
So there is a powerful incentive
to stay away from a limited Aim strategy,

191
00:12:57,943 --> 00:13:04,492
and there's certainly a powerful incentive
to stay away from an attrition strategy.

192
00:13:04,492 --> 00:13:08,245
So when those are the two options
on the table,

193
00:13:08,287 --> 00:13:12,500
you're likely to see deterrence succeed.

194
00:13:12,541 --> 00:13:17,963
It's when an army can launch a blitzkrieg
that deterrence is likely to fail.

195
00:13:18,005 --> 00:13:21,175
So this is my story about

196
00:13:21,175 --> 00:13:24,178
the military variable. Now

197
00:13:25,346 --> 00:13:27,473
as I said earlier,

198
00:13:27,473 --> 00:13:31,602
you have to take into account
the political variable as well.

199
00:13:32,269 --> 00:13:35,856
Now, there's no question that anytime

200
00:13:36,190 --> 00:13:39,401
a country is thinking about going to war,

201
00:13:40,236 --> 00:13:43,447
the political variable

202
00:13:43,447 --> 00:13:46,450
is going to basically say,

203
00:13:47,326 --> 00:13:51,622
we have very good reason to go to war.

204
00:13:52,748 --> 00:13:55,125
By definition, you don't think about

205
00:13:55,125 --> 00:13:59,380
launching an attack against an adversary

206
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:03,175
unless you think there is a strong set

207
00:14:03,175 --> 00:14:06,220
of political reasons for doing so.

208
00:14:07,054 --> 00:14:10,558
Nevertheless, in some cases,

209
00:14:10,558 --> 00:14:15,813
in some cases,
the intensity of the political forces

210
00:14:15,813 --> 00:14:18,816
that are pushing a state to go to war

211
00:14:18,899 --> 00:14:22,361
are extremely great.

212
00:14:22,444 --> 00:14:27,157
That a state feels that it has

213
00:14:27,241 --> 00:14:32,830
to solve the problem that it faces,
it has to solve the political problem

214
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:37,084
that it faces with military force.

215
00:14:37,084 --> 00:14:43,424
And what happens in cases like that
is that states

216
00:14:43,674 --> 00:14:47,511
usually go to a limited aim strategy.

217
00:14:47,761 --> 00:14:52,016
This is
this is the the set of cases where you see

218
00:14:52,057 --> 00:14:55,728
a limited
Aim strategy lead to deterrence failure.

219
00:14:55,769 --> 00:15:01,317
You remember my point when I talked about
the military variable was that

220
00:15:01,358 --> 00:15:07,156
blitzkrieg produces deterrence failures,
attrition strategy, deterrence, success,

221
00:15:07,197 --> 00:15:11,410
limited aim, strategy,
most of the time deterrence, success.

222
00:15:11,452 --> 00:15:15,706
What I'm telling you is
when you factor in the political variable

223
00:15:15,706 --> 00:15:21,253
and there are intense pressures
to go to war and then

224
00:15:22,212 --> 00:15:25,215
the state, if
it doesn't have the capability to launch

225
00:15:25,257 --> 00:15:29,720
a blitzkrieg,
will launch a limited Aim strategy.

226
00:15:29,762 --> 00:15:34,350
And let me give you what I think
are the two classic examples of that.

227
00:15:34,391 --> 00:15:39,772
One is the 1973 Egyptian attack

228
00:15:40,147 --> 00:15:43,901
against the Israelis in the Sinai.

229
00:15:44,109 --> 00:15:47,071
The Egyptians crossed the Suez Canal,

230
00:15:47,071 --> 00:15:51,951
and they grabbed off a piece of territory
in the Sinai

231
00:15:51,992 --> 00:15:57,039
that Israel had controlled up till then,
and then they shifted to the defensive.

232
00:15:57,081 --> 00:16:00,125
It was a clear, limited aim strategy.

233
00:16:00,125 --> 00:16:04,421
Now, why did the Egyptians do that?

234
00:16:04,421 --> 00:16:08,801
On March 8th,
who was then the president of Egypt, felt

235
00:16:08,801 --> 00:16:14,556
that it was absolutely essential
that the Egyptians use military force

236
00:16:14,556 --> 00:16:20,312
against Israel to send a clear message
to Israel that they had they,

237
00:16:20,312 --> 00:16:24,608
meaning the Egyptians had to get back
the Sinai Peninsula.

238
00:16:25,192 --> 00:16:30,698
So they launched in October of 1973.

239
00:16:30,739 --> 00:16:33,742
This limited aims strategy.

240
00:16:33,784 --> 00:16:37,413
They did not launch a blitzkrieg
because there was no way

241
00:16:37,413 --> 00:16:41,291
that the Egyptians could defeat
the Israelis with a blitzkrieg strategy

242
00:16:41,291 --> 00:16:42,584
that was off the table,

243
00:16:42,584 --> 00:16:46,839
and they did not want to engage
in attrition war if they could avoid it.

244
00:16:46,839 --> 00:16:49,967
So they launched a limited Aim strategy.

245
00:16:50,884 --> 00:16:52,594
Same is true with the Japanese.

246
00:16:52,594 --> 00:16:55,764
When they attacked
the United States at Pearl Harbor

247
00:16:55,764 --> 00:17:00,102
in December of 1941,
it was a limited aim strategy.

248
00:17:00,436 --> 00:17:03,772
The Japanese understood full

249
00:17:03,772 --> 00:17:08,110
well that they could not defeat
the United States decisively.

250
00:17:08,444 --> 00:17:13,866
They did not have a strategy
that would produce decisive victory,

251
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:17,244
but nevertheless,
the Japanese felt that they were compelled

252
00:17:17,244 --> 00:17:20,998
to attack the United States because
the United States was strangling them

253
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:25,294
economically, and they had to figure a way
to get out of this

254
00:17:25,335 --> 00:17:29,089
incredibly difficult situation
that they were in.

255
00:17:29,089 --> 00:17:32,259
The United States
would not negotiate with them.

256
00:17:32,259 --> 00:17:34,678
So they decided to launch a war.

257
00:17:34,678 --> 00:17:37,681
And again,
they did not have a blitzkrieg strategy.

258
00:17:37,681 --> 00:17:40,059
They wanted to avoid a war of attrition.

259
00:17:40,059 --> 00:17:43,437
So they launched a limited Aim strategy,
which is what the Pearl

260
00:17:43,437 --> 00:17:46,940
Harbor attack was all about.

261
00:17:47,024 --> 00:17:52,988
So you see, in the case of Egypt and 73
and the Japanese in 1941,

262
00:17:52,988 --> 00:17:57,534
two instances of a state
actually pursuing a limited Aim strategy,

263
00:17:57,576 --> 00:18:02,456
two cases where deterrence broke down
because the attacking forces adopted

264
00:18:02,539 --> 00:18:06,585
the limit, adopted a limited Aim strategy.

265
00:18:06,668 --> 00:18:12,299
But the only reason you got a limited
Aim strategy in those two cases

266
00:18:12,299 --> 00:18:16,303
was because the political forces pushing

267
00:18:16,303 --> 00:18:21,517
each one of those states to go to war
were just so incredibly powerful.

268
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:26,605
But otherwise, as I say, the key
to understanding whether or not deterrence

269
00:18:26,605 --> 00:18:30,484
fails is whether or not a state
that's contemplating war

270
00:18:30,609 --> 00:18:33,570
has a blitzkrieg strategy
available or not.

271
00:18:33,570 --> 00:18:37,991
In other words, whether it can win
a quick and decisive victory.

272
00:18:37,991 --> 00:18:42,579
So this is basically my theory
of conventional deterrence.

273
00:18:43,038 --> 00:18:46,333
Now, what I want to do is switch

274
00:18:46,333 --> 00:18:52,297
gears, and I want to talk
about the Russian decision

275
00:18:52,297 --> 00:18:57,094
to invade Ukraine on February 24th, 2022.

276
00:18:57,094 --> 00:19:00,806
And of course, my argument
is that the Russians

277
00:19:00,806 --> 00:19:03,892
could choose among three strategies.

278
00:19:04,184 --> 00:19:09,565
They could choose a blitzkrieg, 
they could choose an attrition

279
00:19:09,565 --> 00:19:14,069
strategy, or they could choose a limited
aim strategy.

280
00:19:14,111 --> 00:19:15,654
Maybe I shouldn't say they could choose.

281
00:19:15,654 --> 00:19:17,364
They could contemplate.

282
00:19:17,364 --> 00:19:18,782
It's probably a better way to put it.

283
00:19:18,782 --> 00:19:21,326
They could contemplate
a blitzkrieg strategy,

284
00:19:21,326 --> 00:19:24,621
an attrition strategy,
or a limited aim strategy.

285
00:19:24,746 --> 00:19:28,625
Now, the conventional wisdom in the West

286
00:19:28,709 --> 00:19:33,672
is that the Russians
launched a blitzkrieg.

287
00:19:33,922 --> 00:19:35,757
Let me say a bit more about this.

288
00:19:36,884 --> 00:19:38,427
The conventional wisdom in

289
00:19:38,427 --> 00:19:41,763
the West is clearly that

290
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:46,476
Vladimir Putin was an aggressor

291
00:19:46,476 --> 00:19:51,940
who was interested
in conquering all of Ukraine

292
00:19:51,940 --> 00:19:56,862
and incorporating it
into a Greater Russia.

293
00:19:56,904 --> 00:20:00,657
And of course, as you know, 
many people believe that

294
00:20:00,657 --> 00:20:03,827
after he was done with Ukraine,
he would try to conquer

295
00:20:03,827 --> 00:20:08,540
other territory in Eastern Europe
and reestablish the Soviet empire.

296
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:11,585
But that's all beside the point,
because we're simply concerned here

297
00:20:11,585 --> 00:20:17,716
about the question
of why Russia attacked Ukraine.

298
00:20:17,716 --> 00:20:21,887
And again, the goal
so the conventional wisdom goes,

299
00:20:21,887 --> 00:20:26,767
is that Putin wanted to conquer
all of Ukraine.

300
00:20:26,808 --> 00:20:30,062
This is another way of saying that Putin

301
00:20:30,062 --> 00:20:34,316
was pursuing a decisive victory.

302
00:20:34,524 --> 00:20:37,736
And of course, he thought he was going

303
00:20:37,778 --> 00:20:40,989
to win a quick and decisive victory.

304
00:20:41,031 --> 00:20:44,910
He thought that he had this formidable
military instrument,

305
00:20:44,910 --> 00:20:48,580
the Russian army,
that would quickly overrun

306
00:20:48,580 --> 00:20:53,794
the Ukrainian army and up
conquering all of Ukraine

307
00:20:53,794 --> 00:20:58,632
and then incorporating it
into a Greater Russia.

308
00:20:58,674 --> 00:21:01,510
This would be a quick
and decisive victory.

309
00:21:01,510 --> 00:21:05,138
And of course, as I said to you,
when I laid out my theory

310
00:21:05,264 --> 00:21:09,601
that if a state thinks
that it can affect the blitzkrieg,

311
00:21:09,643 --> 00:21:11,436
then deterrence is going to fail.

312
00:21:11,436 --> 00:21:15,565
And if you can,
if you accept the conventional wisdom

313
00:21:15,607 --> 00:21:18,819
about what Putin was up to,

314
00:21:18,860 --> 00:21:23,490
it's obvious why deterrence failed

315
00:21:23,490 --> 00:21:27,452
and why Russia invaded Ukraine.

316
00:21:28,412 --> 00:21:29,162
Now, I

317
00:21:29,162 --> 00:21:32,624
disagree with this argument,
but before I tell you why,

318
00:21:33,166 --> 00:21:37,421
let me note that once
the blitzkrieg failed

319
00:21:37,421 --> 00:21:39,298
and of course the blitzkrieg failed

320
00:21:39,298 --> 00:21:43,510
in the conventional wisdom,
what you got was a war of attrition.

321
00:21:43,510 --> 00:21:46,263
And this was the point that I made to
you earlier.

322
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:50,934
Remember I said, when a blitzkrieg fails,
it leads to a war of attrition.

323
00:21:50,934 --> 00:21:55,188
This is what happened to the Soviets
on the Eastern Front in World War Two.

324
00:21:55,188 --> 00:21:57,816
And so the conventional wisdom goes.

325
00:21:57,816 --> 00:22:01,903
This is what happened to the Russians
efforts.

326
00:22:01,987 --> 00:22:03,947
They invaded Ukraine.

327
00:22:03,947 --> 00:22:06,950
The blitzkrieg failed
and we got a war of attrition.

328
00:22:07,743 --> 00:22:11,705
Now, why is this argument wrong?

329
00:22:11,872 --> 00:22:15,500
First of all, there is no way

330
00:22:15,834 --> 00:22:19,671
that the Russian army that went into
Ukraine

331
00:22:19,713 --> 00:22:24,885
could have come even close
to conquering all of Ukraine.

332
00:22:24,885 --> 00:22:28,388
It was just too small, the Russian army,

333
00:22:28,388 --> 00:22:32,809
and it was not a high quality
military machine.

334
00:22:32,809 --> 00:22:38,899
It was not an army that was well
oiled for offensive operations.

335
00:22:38,899 --> 00:22:43,695
It was an army that was basically built
for a defensive mission.

336
00:22:44,029 --> 00:22:47,449
And furthermore, as I said,
it was a rather small army.

337
00:22:47,574 --> 00:22:52,412
I argue that the Russian army
that went into

338
00:22:52,454 --> 00:22:56,291
Ukraine had at most 190,000

339
00:22:56,583 --> 00:22:59,961
troops, 190,000 general

340
00:23:00,629 --> 00:23:04,633
who is the head of the Ukrainian military

341
00:23:04,633 --> 00:23:10,389
today, argues that the Russians went in
with 100,000 troops.

342
00:23:10,430 --> 00:23:13,433
There is absolutely no way
that the Russians

343
00:23:13,433 --> 00:23:16,812
could even come close
to conquering all of Ukraine

344
00:23:16,812 --> 00:23:21,024
with 100,000 troops,
or even with 190,000 troops.

345
00:23:21,316 --> 00:23:24,069
When the Vermont, the German Army

346
00:23:24,069 --> 00:23:27,155
went into Poland in 1939.

347
00:23:27,155 --> 00:23:31,159
And you want to remember that
the Vermont was only interested

348
00:23:31,159 --> 00:23:34,413
in conquering the western
one half of Poland,

349
00:23:34,413 --> 00:23:39,543
because the Soviet Union was going
to conquer the eastern one half of Poland.

350
00:23:39,626 --> 00:23:44,256
When the Wehrmacht went into western
Poland, it had 1.5

351
00:23:44,256 --> 00:23:48,218
million men, 1.5 million,

352
00:23:48,218 --> 00:23:53,181
and the western half of Poland
is much smaller than Ukraine.

353
00:23:53,181 --> 00:23:58,770
And again, general says the Russians
went in with 100,000 troops.

354
00:23:58,770 --> 00:24:02,816
I say they win in with at most 190,000
troops.

355
00:24:02,816 --> 00:24:03,650
And again,

356
00:24:03,650 --> 00:24:09,990
this was not an army that was well suited
for offensive military operations.

357
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:13,368
To put it in slightly different terms,
this was not the Wehrmacht.

358
00:24:13,410 --> 00:24:19,166
The Wehrmacht was a formidable
fighting force that was top notch

359
00:24:19,166 --> 00:24:22,919
when it came to executing
offensive armored operations.

360
00:24:22,919 --> 00:24:25,255
That was not the Russian army.

361
00:24:25,255 --> 00:24:29,217
So my first argument
against the conventional wisdom,

362
00:24:29,217 --> 00:24:32,304
and again, the conventional wisdom, is
that Russia was bent

363
00:24:32,304 --> 00:24:36,141
on achieving
a quick and decisive victory,

364
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:40,479
is that they just didn't have an army
that was big enough to achieve that goal.

365
00:24:40,687 --> 00:24:45,817
Second point I would make to you
is that one day after the offensive

366
00:24:45,817 --> 00:24:49,905
started on February 25th, Dmitry Peskov,

367
00:24:49,946 --> 00:24:53,158
who was at the time

368
00:24:54,993 --> 00:24:56,119
Putin's principal

369
00:24:56,119 --> 00:25:00,415
spokesman, reached out to the Ukrainians
and said that

370
00:25:00,415 --> 00:25:04,878
the Russians were interested
in starting negotiations.

371
00:25:04,961 --> 00:25:06,630
Just think about this.

372
00:25:06,630 --> 00:25:12,802
One day after the Russians start the war,
the Russians reach out to the Ukrainians

373
00:25:12,802 --> 00:25:18,642
and say they'd like to start negotiations
to put an end to the war.

374
00:25:18,975 --> 00:25:23,480
This does not fit with the story
that Putin was bent on conquering

375
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:27,651
all of Ukraine and incorporating it
in to a greater Russia.

376
00:25:27,776 --> 00:25:31,988
Then what happens over
the course of March?

377
00:25:32,906 --> 00:25:36,952
This is March 2022,
right after the war starts.

378
00:25:36,993 --> 00:25:43,291
Is that the Ukrainians
and the Russians begin to negotiate

379
00:25:43,291 --> 00:25:47,003
with each other,
and eventually what happens

380
00:25:47,003 --> 00:25:50,757
is on 29th March.

381
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:56,263
On 29th March, there's a little over
one month after the war starts.

382
00:25:56,346 --> 00:26:01,810
Negotiations
to end the war start in Istanbul.

383
00:26:01,851 --> 00:26:06,356
These are the famous Istanbul negotiations
that are designed to shut down the war.

384
00:26:06,815 --> 00:26:11,945
Furthermore, and this is very important
as a goodwill gesture,

385
00:26:12,070 --> 00:26:16,241
the Russians evacuate their military

386
00:26:16,241 --> 00:26:19,244
from around the key region

387
00:26:19,536 --> 00:26:23,081
and they, in effect,

388
00:26:23,123 --> 00:26:26,334
turn the key region over to Ukraine.

389
00:26:27,210 --> 00:26:31,047
Again,
this does not sound like the Russians,

390
00:26:31,047 --> 00:26:35,719
and in particular,
Vladimir Putin, were bent on conquering

391
00:26:36,177 --> 00:26:40,015
all of Ukraine,
winning a decisive victory.

392
00:26:40,015 --> 00:26:44,978
And in fact,
what happens after 19th March in Istanbul

393
00:26:44,978 --> 00:26:49,733
is that the two sides
do begin to negotiate, and it's very clear

394
00:26:49,733 --> 00:26:53,945
that there are a lot
of difficult issues on the table,

395
00:26:54,195 --> 00:26:57,490
and it's not clear
you would have ever gotten an agreement.

396
00:26:57,490 --> 00:27:01,161
But the central agreement on the table

397
00:27:01,161 --> 00:27:04,789
is the question of Ukrainian neutrality.

398
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:09,544
And what was going on here
is that the Russians just wanted

399
00:27:09,544 --> 00:27:13,298
to make sure
that Ukraine did not join NATO.

400
00:27:13,340 --> 00:27:16,676
They were not interested
in conquering all of Ukraine.

401
00:27:16,718 --> 00:27:19,763
They did walk, did not want Ukraine

402
00:27:19,763 --> 00:27:23,183
to turn into a Western bulwark
on that on their border.

403
00:27:23,183 --> 00:27:26,478
And that's what
this operation was all about.

404
00:27:26,519 --> 00:27:28,396
I'll talk more about this in a second.

405
00:27:28,396 --> 00:27:31,399
But the point I'm trying to make to you

406
00:27:31,441 --> 00:27:34,527
is that given
the size of the Russian army,

407
00:27:34,527 --> 00:27:38,698
given the quality of the Russian army,
and given the fact that the Russians

408
00:27:38,698 --> 00:27:43,036
reach out to the Ukrainians
to start negotiations right after the war

409
00:27:43,078 --> 00:27:45,455
starts, and then given the fact that

410
00:27:45,455 --> 00:27:49,417
those negotiations actually started
a little over a month later,

411
00:27:49,626 --> 00:27:54,422
it's, in my opinion, almost impossible
to sustain the story

412
00:27:54,422 --> 00:27:59,719
that the Russians were interested
in conquering Ukraine.

413
00:27:59,761 --> 00:28:04,766
My final point on this is those
negotiations eventually break down.

414
00:28:04,766 --> 00:28:07,977
And as I said,
I'm not sure that they ever would have

415
00:28:07,977 --> 00:28:11,648
produced a final agreement
that would have ended the war back then.

416
00:28:11,648 --> 00:28:16,695
But it's not the Russians
who walk away from the negotiations.

417
00:28:16,695 --> 00:28:20,240
I think it's the Ukrainians
who bear the principal responsibility

418
00:28:20,281 --> 00:28:23,284
from walking away from the negotiations.

419
00:28:23,368 --> 00:28:26,955
And of course, what happens then?

420
00:28:27,330 --> 00:28:32,836
What's
what's the war is not settled in Istanbul,

421
00:28:32,877 --> 00:28:36,339
is it evolves into a war of attrition.

422
00:28:37,090 --> 00:28:38,007
Okay.

423
00:28:38,007 --> 00:28:42,387
Let me give you my view, using my theory

424
00:28:42,387 --> 00:28:48,351
on what happened
that led to the war on February

425
00:28:48,351 --> 00:28:54,274
24th, 2022,
the Russians did not launch a blitzkrieg.

426
00:28:54,858 --> 00:28:57,318
I've just tried to give you some evidence.

427
00:28:57,318 --> 00:29:00,697
There's more, but I gave you
some of the principal evidence

428
00:29:00,697 --> 00:29:05,452
that shows you
this was not a case of Vladimir Putin

429
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:09,831
turning his army loose to conquer
all of Ukraine.

430
00:29:09,873 --> 00:29:14,210
Know what Putin did is he launched
the limited Aim strategy.

431
00:29:14,252 --> 00:29:19,549
If you go into Ukraine with 100,000
or 190,000 troops and you withdraw

432
00:29:19,549 --> 00:29:24,220
from Kyiv as soon as the negotiations
in Istanbul starts,

433
00:29:24,304 --> 00:29:27,724
you're pursuing a limited
Aim strategy here.

434
00:29:27,974 --> 00:29:31,936
What Putin did was Putin went into Ukraine

435
00:29:31,936 --> 00:29:36,524
and he captured a small chunk,

436
00:29:37,233 --> 00:29:39,652
a small chunk of territory

437
00:29:39,652 --> 00:29:42,655
inside of Ukraine.

438
00:29:42,781 --> 00:29:46,034
And he then told

439
00:29:46,075 --> 00:29:49,454
the Ukrainians
that he wanted to start negotiating.

440
00:29:49,913 --> 00:29:51,748
What's going on here?

441
00:29:51,748 --> 00:29:55,710
What's going on here is that Putin

442
00:29:55,710 --> 00:29:59,881
believed that NATO's policy

443
00:29:59,881 --> 00:30:05,804
of trying to incorporate Ukraine
into the Western alliance,

444
00:30:06,262 --> 00:30:09,265
the West policy of trying

445
00:30:09,265 --> 00:30:12,811
to make Ukraine a western bulwark

446
00:30:12,977 --> 00:30:18,066
on Russia's
border, was an existential threat.

447
00:30:18,441 --> 00:30:22,070
The Russians were perfectly clear
on this issue

448
00:30:22,070 --> 00:30:27,450
from April 8th,
excuse me, from April 2008 forward.

449
00:30:27,450 --> 00:30:31,871
And of course, April 2008 is
when NATO says that Ukraine

450
00:30:31,871 --> 00:30:36,084
will become part of the alliance
from April 2008 forward.

451
00:30:36,084 --> 00:30:41,589
The Russians are clear this is an existential threat, and it's not going to happen

452
00:30:43,216 --> 00:30:46,302
in December of 2021,

453
00:30:46,678 --> 00:30:51,724
January of 2022 and in February of 2022.

454
00:30:51,724 --> 00:30:55,436
These are the three months before the war
starts.

455
00:30:56,396 --> 00:30:59,983
The Russians go to enormous lengths to try

456
00:31:00,024 --> 00:31:04,445
to negotiate with the West to avoid a war.

457
00:31:04,737 --> 00:31:08,449
The Russians are not interested in war.

458
00:31:08,575 --> 00:31:10,159
This is perfectly clear.

459
00:31:10,159 --> 00:31:14,581
If you look at the historical record for
those three months before the war started.

460
00:31:14,914 --> 00:31:21,087
Nevertheless,
the West has zero interest in negotiating

461
00:31:21,129 --> 00:31:24,632
with the Russians to make sure

462
00:31:24,632 --> 00:31:27,760
that a war does not break out over
Ukraine.

463
00:31:27,802 --> 00:31:30,305
Just go look at the historical record.

464
00:31:30,305 --> 00:31:34,017
You'll find hardly
any evidence that the West,

465
00:31:34,017 --> 00:31:36,644
which believe
that the Russians might attack.

466
00:31:36,644 --> 00:31:39,689
You want to understand
that many people in the West thought

467
00:31:39,689 --> 00:31:42,984
that there was a good chance
that the Russians would attack.

468
00:31:43,026 --> 00:31:45,320
Nevertheless,
the West and here I'm talking

469
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:50,408
mainly about the United States,
did virtually nothing to prevent a war.

470
00:31:51,451 --> 00:31:54,495
What this
meant was that Putin was desperate.

471
00:31:54,871 --> 00:31:58,499
He believed that he faced, well,

472
00:31:58,499 --> 00:32:01,711
I should say his country
faced an existential threat.

473
00:32:01,711 --> 00:32:04,547
And he had to deal
with this existential threat.

474
00:32:04,547 --> 00:32:10,511
He fully understand, fully understood
that he had no blitzkrieg option.

475
00:32:10,511 --> 00:32:12,764
That was not in the cards.

476
00:32:12,764 --> 00:32:15,725
Putin was not

477
00:32:15,725 --> 00:32:18,519
Putin was not running the Vermont.

478
00:32:18,519 --> 00:32:20,897
The Russian army was not the Vermont.

479
00:32:20,897 --> 00:32:24,859
The Russian Army was a defensively
oriented army, small in number.

480
00:32:24,859 --> 00:32:28,821
It had no capability
of winning a decisive victory in Ukraine.

481
00:32:28,821 --> 00:32:31,741
So he pursued a limited aim strategy.

482
00:32:31,741 --> 00:32:34,619
This is exactly
what my theory would predict.

483
00:32:34,619 --> 00:32:38,873
The political variable here,
the political forces

484
00:32:38,873 --> 00:32:43,378
that were pushing Putin to go to war
were extremely powerful.

485
00:32:43,419 --> 00:32:47,006
Again, Putin felt
that Russia faced an existential threat.

486
00:32:47,048 --> 00:32:48,883
He had to deal with that threat.

487
00:32:48,883 --> 00:32:51,844
And the United States
and the Europeans were doing

488
00:32:51,844 --> 00:32:55,974
nothing to negotiate a way to avoid war.

489
00:32:55,974 --> 00:32:58,768
So he felt he had to use military force.

490
00:32:58,768 --> 00:33:01,813
And as I've said,
he had no blitzkrieg option.

491
00:33:01,813 --> 00:33:05,775
So what he did
was he launched a limited Aim strategy.

492
00:33:05,775 --> 00:33:09,570
And if you look at the size of the Army,
and if you look at his interest

493
00:33:09,612 --> 00:33:14,283
in sending a signal to the Ukrainians
to, let's start negotiating

494
00:33:14,325 --> 00:33:18,204
right after the war starts,
you see clearly that

495
00:33:18,204 --> 00:33:21,207
this is what he had in mind.

496
00:33:21,749 --> 00:33:26,004
However, as I told you
at the beginning of the lecture,

497
00:33:26,004 --> 00:33:31,676
the problem of the limited aim strategy
is that the other side,

498
00:33:32,051 --> 00:33:36,347
the target state, is unlikely
to roll over and play dead.

499
00:33:36,347 --> 00:33:42,729
It's unlikely to surrender, and indeed
it's likely to continue the fight.

500
00:33:42,729 --> 00:33:48,901
And in this case, Ukraine,
with help from the West.

501
00:33:48,943 --> 00:33:51,487
This includes the Europeans
and the United States.

502
00:33:51,487 --> 00:33:55,616
And we're talking about, with abundant
help from the West,

503
00:33:55,658 --> 00:33:58,661
the Ukrainians continue

504
00:33:58,661 --> 00:34:02,457
the fight against Russia.

505
00:34:02,498 --> 00:34:05,376
I believe that
one of the principal reasons

506
00:34:05,376 --> 00:34:09,797
that the Ukrainians walk away
from the Istanbul negotiations,

507
00:34:09,797 --> 00:34:13,885
and I believe that the West encourages
them to do that.

508
00:34:13,885 --> 00:34:17,722
To do
this is because they believe that Ukraine,

509
00:34:17,722 --> 00:34:20,808
with help from the West,
can prevail over the Russians

510
00:34:20,975 --> 00:34:25,396
in the long term because the Russian army
is not that strong,

511
00:34:25,813 --> 00:34:28,941
which, of course, is why
it pursued a limited Aim strategy.

512
00:34:28,941 --> 00:34:32,361
So the Ukrainians,
with help from the West, continue the war.

513
00:34:32,361 --> 00:34:34,614
And what's the end result?

514
00:34:34,614 --> 00:34:40,828
The end result is that this limited aim
strategy turns into a war of attrition.

515
00:34:40,828 --> 00:34:44,290
And of course, as all the viewers know,
we are now

516
00:34:44,373 --> 00:34:49,670
engaged in a war of attrition with Russia

517
00:34:49,712 --> 00:34:53,716
on one side, Ukraine
and the West on the other side.

518
00:34:53,966 --> 00:34:58,304
So to sum up here,
when you think about what happened

519
00:34:58,304 --> 00:35:03,476
on February 24th, 2022,
what you want to do

520
00:35:03,476 --> 00:35:08,231
is think about this as a case of a failure
of conventional deterrence.

521
00:35:08,523 --> 00:35:12,944
And as I said before, when you think
about conventional deterrence,

522
00:35:13,236 --> 00:35:17,156
you have to think about the political
variable and the military variable.

523
00:35:17,156 --> 00:35:19,033
And when you think
about the military variable,

524
00:35:19,033 --> 00:35:23,871
there are three strategies blitzkrieg,
attrition, and limited aim strategy.

525
00:35:23,871 --> 00:35:27,083
And if a potential attacker can pursue

526
00:35:27,083 --> 00:35:30,461
a blitzkrieg
strategy, deterrence is likely to fail.

527
00:35:30,461 --> 00:35:32,004
And the only time

528
00:35:32,004 --> 00:35:36,008
I think you're going to get a state
pursuing a limited Aim strategy,

529
00:35:36,008 --> 00:35:39,887
which is the second best alternative,
but not a great alternative,

530
00:35:39,929 --> 00:35:44,892
is when the political forces
that call for war are so powerful

531
00:35:44,892 --> 00:35:49,272
that a state is willing to risk it
and pursue a limited Aim strategy.

532
00:35:49,438 --> 00:35:54,902
And I think what happened
in the case of the Ukraine war

533
00:35:54,944 --> 00:35:59,157
is that the Russians
did not have a blitzkrieg strategy.

534
00:35:59,157 --> 00:36:04,745
I know that's the conventional wisdom, but
they did not have a blitzkrieg strategy.

535
00:36:04,871 --> 00:36:07,623
They pursued a limited aim strategy.

536
00:36:07,623 --> 00:36:12,003
And the reason they pursued limited
Aim strategy is

537
00:36:12,003 --> 00:36:16,132
because they thought
that they faced an existential threat,

538
00:36:16,174 --> 00:36:19,385
that they had to deal with that threat,

539
00:36:19,385 --> 00:36:24,015
and they could not deal with the threat
through negotiations

540
00:36:24,015 --> 00:36:28,144
with the West,
because the West would just not negotiate.

541
00:36:28,144 --> 00:36:30,396
So they had to use military force.

542
00:36:30,396 --> 00:36:35,860
And they launched this limited attack 
on February

543
00:36:35,860 --> 00:36:41,073
24th, 2022, which at first began
to look like it might work.

544
00:36:41,157 --> 00:36:46,537
You want to remember you got negotiations
at Istanbul, and there are many people

545
00:36:46,537 --> 00:36:50,750
who do think there was a good chance
those negotiations would have worked

546
00:36:50,833 --> 00:36:55,755
had the West not encouraged
the Ukrainians to walk away.

547
00:36:56,088 --> 00:37:00,551
But the negotiations failed,
and here we are

548
00:37:01,093 --> 00:37:04,639
in this lengthy war of attrition.

549
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:09,435
And as I said, this is exactly
what my argument would expect.

550
00:37:09,936 --> 00:37:12,688
So that's my story on

551
00:37:12,688 --> 00:37:16,150
the origins and also on the

552
00:37:17,485 --> 00:37:19,111
conduct of

553
00:37:19,111 --> 00:37:24,283
the Ukraine war since February 24th, 2022.

554
00:37:24,408 --> 00:37:26,869
Thank you very much for your attention.

